<TeXmacs|2.1.5>

<style|article>

<\body>
  <section|Build a matrix>

  This is a working manuscript. Click anywhere in the lesson and edit it as
  you go. The matrix below is made from native TeXmacs cells, not typed text.

  <subsection|Worked example>

  <\equation*>
    A = <matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|<frac|x|2>>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|1>>>>>
  </equation*>

  The outer structure is a matrix. Inside it are two rows, and each row
  contains two editable cells. The fraction in the upper-right cell is nested
  inside the matrix cell.

  <subsection|Make one yourself>

  1. Place the cursor in this paragraph and choose Insert, then Mathematics,
  then Displayed formula.

  2. With the cursor in the new formula, choose Insert, then Table, then
  Matrix. Start with the default grid.

  3. Type in the first cell. Press Tab to move to the next cell. Use the arrow
  keys to move between cells and the surrounding equation.

  4. In one cell, insert a fraction or superscript from Insert, then
  Mathematics. Click directly in its numerator or exponent to edit that
  nested part.

  <subsection|Try this>

  Change the worked matrix into a 3 by 3 identity matrix. Then replace the
  middle entry with a fraction. Use Source mode to inspect the nested row,
  cell, fraction, and equation tags.

  <strong|Keep experimenting:> rename this lesson, add another equation, or
  delete these instructions. It is your editable copy.
</body>
